2026-11-25 (before any screening, translation or scoring): at the user's request, round 2 uses the
first 50 of the 100 fetched repos (alphabetical by owner__repo, the order extraction runs in; not
content-based) instead of all 100. If the kept count from these 50 is low, the preregistered
synthetic fill tops up to 50 new rules. The other 50 repos stay downloaded and unused.
Correction (same day, before screening): the 50 repos are not the first 50 alphabetically.
Extraction re-sorted on each pass while repos were still arriving, so the set is the first 50 repos
extracted (close to the scraper's seeded fetch order); still not content-based. List:
data/runs/boundary2/repos_used.txt. Extraction: 1,367 files, 9,901 rules, 9 dropped (non-verbatim quotes).
Sampling rule fixed before screening: the 50 repos hold 9,901 rules (round 1: 1,287), so the kept set
may be large. All kept rules count toward coverage; if more than 50 real rules are kept, a seeded
random sample of 50 (random.Random(2520), sorted (folder, id) list) gets actions, labels and arms.
Codex screening runs in batches of 5 folders (about 1,000 rules per call, round 1's size).
Screening retry (mechanical): batch04's answer covered 244 of its 3,486 rules (one folder holds
2,533). The 3,242 unjudged rules (4 folders) are re-screened with the same prompt in chunks of 700
(select/retry*.md). No judged rule is re-asked.
Retry dropped (user request, to move fast): retry01 ran 30+ min with no answer and was stopped.
The 3,242 unjudged rules (4 folders: OpenWorshipApp 2,533, OwlsNightCatch 616, ggOceanMaps 62,
glute 31) are left out. Coverage denominator = the 6,659 rules Codex actually judged.
Two stages (user request, before any translation result was seen): stage 1 = rules from
translation batches 01-03 (the first 150 eligible rules in sorted folder order); after the filter
and both exactness reviews, up to 25 kept rules (seeded sample, random.Random(2520), if more) get
actions, labels and arms and are scored as an interim result. Stage 2 = batches 04-10, up to 25
kept rules the same way. The final result pools both stages (<= 50 rules); the interim is labelled
interim and never replaces it.
Bug (plumbing): codex exec reads stdin when it is not closed; runs started from the first job
inherited an open stdin and hung ("Reading additional input from stdin"), which is also why retry01
hung. Fix: stdin=DEVNULL in boundary2.codex(). Hung translate batches 01-07 were killed (no output)
and rerun; batches 08-10 had completed normally.
Order (to move fast): stage-1 actions, rules and translations frozen (freeze_s1_actions.sha256) before
labels finish; arms run in parallel with labelling. Arm outputs are not opened until both label sets
are in and frozen (freeze_s1_labels.sha256). Labelers never see arm outputs.
Stopped at stage 1 (user decision, 2026-11-25): Codex hit its spend cap again after all stage-1 Codex
work had finished (no cap errors in any stage-1 log). Stage 2's 25 sampled rules and 200 actions were
written and blinded (actions_s2.json, actions_blind_s2.json) but never labelled or judged. The round-2
result is stage 1 (13 rules, 104 actions); coverage uses both stages' screening (58 kept / 6,659).
The pooled round 1 + 2 analysis was not run.
